ltsp (6.0.0-5) trixie; urgency=medium

  * The provisioning tools under server/tools are now installed into
    /usr/sbin, each with a manual page in section 8: build-apt-repo,
    build-autoinstall-iso, provision-samba-ad-dc, setup-home-server,
    setup-lb-addressing, setup-ltsp-app and setup-ltsp-root. They existed
    only in the git tree until now, so anyone installing ltsp-server from
    the archive got the documentation's subject but not its object.
  * The manual pages carry the findings, not just the syntax: why the
    domain join uses adcli rather than realmd, why sssd needs
    ad_gpo_map_remote_interactive before a correct password is accepted,
    why an orphaned xfce4-session makes every later login fail silently,
    why the offload unit is useless without ethtool installed, and why a
    keepalived pair on DHCP takes itself apart.
  * HOWTO-lab-from-scratch and PXE-network-boot-test are installed into
    /usr/share/doc/ltsp-server; they were written but never shipped.
  * The preseeds are installed into /usr/share/ltsp-server/preseed as
    reference material for build-autoinstall-iso(8).
  * RDP_PREAUTH=True now selects the method rather than meaning Kerberos
    (ldm >= 3.0.3-1): a configured realm picks Kerberos, no realm picks the
    ssh probe, and False still means no check at all. Documented with the
    one asymmetry that matters - the automatic fallback to ssh runs only
    where the host keys are present, because the probe does not fail open
    on an unverified key and an upgrade must not turn a working login into
    a locked one.
  * lts.conf(5) documents RDP_PREAUTH=ssh, RDP_PREAUTH_HOST and
    RDP_PREAUTH_SSH_KNOWN_HOSTS (ldm >= 3.0.3-1): the credential check for
    sites with local accounts and no KDC. It records what that variant
    costs as well as what it buys - it sends the password to the host it
    asks, where Kerberos does not, so the host keys belong in the image
    via ltsp-update-sshkeys(8), which copies ssh_host_*_key.pub and
    nothing secret. RDP_PREAUTH_HOST exists because HAProxy forwards 3389
    only: ssh to the load balancer address would reach the balancer, not a
    session server.
  * ltsp-swapfile-delete(8) is documented too - it was the last command in
    the package without a manual page. Writing it turned up a real fault
    worth recording: the script ends by calling /usr/sbin/tmpwatch, a Red
    Hat tool that Debian does not package at all (the equivalent being
    tmpreaper), and then exits 0 regardless. On Debian it marks the live
    clients and deletes nothing, while cron records a success - so the swap
    directory only ever grows and no error explains it. The BUGS section
    gives the tmpreaper invocation to replace it with.
  * New: lts.conf(5). The example file has always pointed at "your installed
    lts.conf man page" and this fork never shipped one. It documents the
    directives added or changed here - LDM_DEFAULT_BACKEND and
    LDM_FORCE_BACKEND with their precedence, RDP_SERVER, RDP_OPTIONS,
    RDP_PREAUTH and RDP_PREAUTH_REALM, XKBLAYOUT with CONSOLE_KEYMAP,
    SCREEN_nn against ldm.service and ltsp-screen@, X_COLOR_DEPTH,
    LTSP_FATCLIENT, KEEP_SYSTEM_SERVICES and LDM_NBD_CHECKUPDATE - and
    points at the example file for the upstream remainder.
  * server/configs/lts.conf documents the directives added since 5.18:
    LDM_DEFAULT_BACKEND, RDP_SERVER, RDP_OPTIONS, RDP_PREAUTH,
    RDP_PREAUTH_REALM, LDM_NBD_CHECKUPDATE and KEEP_SYSTEM_SERVICES, plus
    CONSOLE_KEYMAP next to XKBLAYOUT - a layout mismatch mistypes the
    password and reads as a rejected login - and a note on SCREEN_07
    against ldm.service and ltsp-screen@.

 -- Marc-Andre Beckmann-Butz <ma@butz.online>  Fri, 11 Sep 2026 21:30:00 +0200

ltsp (6.0.0-4) trixie; urgency=medium

  * ltsp-screen@.service is shipped but no longer enabled on tty1. The
    greeter is started by ldm.service (ldm >= 3.0.2-1), which runs
    screen_session 07 for the SCREEN_07=ldm default that
    ltsp_config.d/15default-screen already sets; enabling an instance here
    as well put a second greeter on tty1 and had the two fighting over
    which VT owns the session. The template stays for the cases it is
    actually useful for - a second screen on another VT, or a screen
    script other than ldm - and is enabled by hand.

 -- Marc-Andre Beckmann-Butz <ma@butz.online>  Thu, 10 Sep 2026 17:50:00 +0200

ltsp (6.0.0-3) trixie; urgency=medium

  * Added ltsp-screen@.service, enabled on tty1. LTSP5 shipped this
    integration in the ltsp-client package, which ltsp6 does not build:
    nothing read SCREEN_nn from lts.conf and nothing launched the matching
    screen.d script, so a client booted correctly all the way to a text
    login with no error anywhere. Two details in the unit are load-bearing
    and each fails differently - the instance is the tty number while
    screen_session is handed it as 0%i (it accepts only [0-1][0-9], and
    /dev/tty01 does not exist, which systemd reports as 208/STDIN), and
    StandardInput=tty is required because screen-x-common derives DISPLAY
    and the vt number from $(tty).
  * ConditionPathExists=/etc/lts.conf keeps the unit inert on a host that
    has the package but no LTSP configuration, rather than respawning
    screen_session against an unset SCREEN_01.

 -- Marc-Andre Beckmann-Butz <ma@butz.online>  Thu, 10 Sep 2026 17:20:00 +0200

ltsp (6.0.0-2) trixie; urgency=medium

  * Initial Debian packaging, split into two binary packages:
    - ltsp-server: ltsp-build-client, ltsp-config, ltsp-chroot,
      ltsp-info, ltsp-update-image/-kernels/-sshkeys, nbdrootd,
      nbdswapd, the /usr/share/ltsp plugin tree, and example
      configs/docs for the HAProxy + keepalived RDP load-balancer
      recipe.
    - ltsp-client-core: init-ltsp, getltscfg (compiled from its
      flex/bison sources), getltscfg-cluster, jetpipe, nbd-proxy
      (built from its own bundled autotools sub-project), the
      localapps/remoteapps helpers and their ldm rc.d hooks, udev
      rules, and the Debian initramfs-tools hooks/scripts needed to
      network-boot a client image.
  * Added Kea DHCPv4 support to ltsp-config (server/configs/kea-dhcp4.conf,
    "ltsp-config kea").
  * Fixed a shell syntax typo ("ase" instead of "case") in the
    ltsp-build-client Debian/013-security-mirror plugin that broke
    security-mirror configuration on every build.
  * Added a "toram" boot option (new local-bottom initramfs script,
    client/Debian/share/initramfs-tools/scripts/local-bottom/nbd_toram):
    copies the NBD root image into a RAM-backed tmpfs after boot and
    disconnects NBD entirely, so a client has no further network/server
    dependency once running. See server/doc/NBD-toram for the full
    write-up, including how to build/serve independent thin- and
    fat-client images side by side via ltsp-build-client --chroot.
  * Fixed two bugs in client/Debian/share/initramfs-tools/hooks/ltsp
    found while packaging and testing on a real Debian 13 kernel:
    - it requested the "overlayfs" and "aufs" kernel modules, neither
      of which exist on any current kernel (renamed to "overlay" resp.
      never mainlined); under this script's "set -e" that aborted the
      whole hook and broke update-initramfs on every install.
    - ltsp-client-core was missing the nbd-client dependency and the
      nbd-client-proxy wrapper script that this same hook requires at
      initramfs-generation time.
  * Verified end-to-end on Debian 13 (trixie): ltsp-build-client builds
    a client chroot, ltsp-client-core and ldm 3.0.0 install into it
    cleanly, update-initramfs succeeds, ltsp-update-image produces a
    working 638 MiB squashfs image, and the toram mechanism was tested
    against that real image over a live NBD connection (copy to RAM,
    then full nbd-client disconnect and nbd-server shutdown, content
    still intact and executable afterward).

 -- Marc-André Beckmann-Butz <ma@butz.online>  Mon, 31 Aug 2026 12:00:00 +0200
